# Internship Progress Log – IIT Jodhpur (Word Restoration & Inpainting + OCR)

**Intern Name:** Sagar  
**Project Duration:** 17 May 2025 – 17 July 2025  
**Mentor:** Dr. Anand Mishra, Assistant Professor and Head of the Vision, Language, and Learning Group (VL2G), IIT Jodhpur  
**Objective:** Generate synthetic word images in 15 Indian languages and evaluate OCR performance using baseline models.

---

## Progress Timeline

### May 25 – June 5
- Set up working environment and installed necessary packages.
- Started with ImageMagick for image generation, but faced issues rendering Hindi characters.
- Switched to PIL (Python Imaging Library) to support multilingual text rendering.
- Collected and filtered suitable fonts for multiple Indian languages.
- Created a basic image generation pipeline with text rendering and placement.
- Generated initial dataset: 10,000 word images per language (5 languages) with simple augmentations.

---

### June 6 – 12
- Created modular `synthetic-data-generation` code.
- Initially applied random combinations of 1–2 noise types per image.
- Used Indic-OCR toolkit (developed by IIT Jodhpur) for evaluation on Indian scripts.
- Tested Tesseract (v5.3) baseline on synthetic data.
- Accuracy (word level):
  - Hindi: 74%
  - Tamil: 69%
  - Telugu: 65%
- Observed that OCR struggled with distorted or curved fonts.

---

### June 13 – 18
- Introduced EasyOCR as a secondary baseline.
- Found better performance for Telugu and Tamil compared to Tesseract.
- Refined image resizing, padding, and alignment logic.
- Categorized noise into three types after initial trials:
  - Pixel Destruction Noise (e.g., salt & pepper, missing pixels)
  - Artifact Noise (e.g., blur, compression, distortions)
  - Environmental/Weather Noise (e.g., fog, shadows, lighting)
- Generated 300,000 images across 10 languages using combined noise types.

---

### June 19 – 25
- Implemented CRNN model using PyTorch.
- Trained CRNN on 200,000 synthetic Hindi word images.
- Compared results with existing OCR engines.
- Character Error Rate (CER) comparison:
  - Tesseract: 24%
  - EasyOCR: 19%
  - CRNN: 12%
- Tested TrOCR (transformer-based OCR) — found it supports English only.
- Plan to extend evaluation to scanned documents and handwritten forms.

